-- ============================================================
-- Warrior (Arms): Juggernaut -- make Charge actually usable in combat
-- ============================================================
-- TODO.md: "Juggernaut (0, 7): Your Charge ability is now usable while in
--           combat. ..."
--
-- woa_2026_08_07_05.sql assumed the stock data path would deliver this, and on
-- paper it does: Charge carries SPELL_ATTR0_NOT_IN_COMBAT_ONLY_PEACEFUL
-- (0x10000000) and Juggernaut carries SPELL_AURA_ABILITY_IGNORE_AURASTATE with
-- EffectMiscValue 1 and a class mask that matches Charge, which is exactly what
-- Spell::CheckCast reads at Spell.cpp:5731-5744 to clear reqCombat before the
-- block at 5761-5762.  Every field of that was re-verified against the built
-- DBC and is correct, and it still failed in game with
-- SPELL_FAILED_AFFECTING_COMBAT ("You are in combat").
--
-- So the gate stops being implicit.  This file removes 0x10000000 from all
-- three Charge ranks -- SpellInfo::CanBeUsedInCombat() then returns true and
-- line 5762 no longer applies to Charge -- and spell_warr_charge_in_combat
-- (WarriorChargeInCombat.cpp) re-imposes the same restriction for warriors
-- without the talent.
--
-- The attribute has to come out of the DBC rather than the script granting an
-- exemption, because CallScriptCheckCastHandlers() runs at Spell.cpp:6046,
-- nearly 300 lines *after* the combat check.  A CheckCast handler can add a
-- failure; it can never clear one.
--
-- 805634064 - 0x10000000 = 537198608.  Nothing else in the attribute mask
-- changes, and no other column on these rows is touched.
--
-- SpellDescription0 still reads "Cannot be used in combat."  Left alone on
-- purpose: it stays true for anyone without Juggernaut, and it is Blizzard's
-- own wording for the same situation.
--
-- Spells:
--   100 / 6178 / 11578 = Charge ranks 1-3
--   64976              = Juggernaut (read by the script, not modified here)
-- ============================================================


